Q: fatal: unable to read tree error on git checkout A: <p>I figured out my problem.  Same message different cause.</p>
<p>This error happens when a remote branch name is the first few letters of a blob hash.  I used 4-6 letters.</p>
<p>To duplicate</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>git cat-file --batch-check --batch-all-objects | grep blob
# Pick the first few letters of any blob hash
git switch -c &lt;FirstFewLettersOfAnyBlobHash&gt;
git push -u origin &lt;FirstFewLettersOfAnyBlobHash&gt; 

# Go somewhere else and clone the repo again
git switch &lt;FirstFewLettersOfAnyBlobHash&gt;
# fatal: unable to read tree  &lt;BlobHash&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The way around it is pick an alternative name and delete the old one.</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>git checkout origin/&lt;FirstFewLettersOfAnyBlobHash&gt;
git switch -c &lt;AnotherName&gt;
git push origin &lt;AnotherName&gt;
git push origin --delete &lt;FirstFewLettersOfAnyBlobHash&gt;
</code></pre>
